reat" due to its nature, not because it will delete your files. In all these cases, the user might recognize the item and say, "Wait, I installed that! It's not a *threat* to me." Quarantine lets them make that call, because the AV's blanket label of "threat" was based on a generic rule, not a personal understanding of the user's intent. So, to answer your question directly: **The word "threat" in this context doesn't mean "proven dangerous by definition" the way it would in a dictionary.** It means "a file that has been flagged by the detection engine as a potential danger." Quarantine exists precisely because that label is a proactive warning, not an infallible identification. If the software could perfectly know it was definitely malicious every single time, there would be no false positives, and then your logic would be right—quarantine would be pointless and deletion would be safe. But because the label itself is an educated guess, the safety net remains essential.
