00:12:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair point to bring up, and I understand why reading something on a tech support site would solidify your view. If an authoritative-sounding source says “flagged files are malware, plain and simple,” it’s natural to take that at face value. The catch is that **tech support sites often simplify things for a mainstream audience who just wants a quick, actionable answer**, not the full technical picture. Their goal is to give clear advice: “If your AV says it’s a virus, trust it and remove it.” That’s good, safe guidance for 99% of home users dealing with an obvious infection. But “plain and simple” advice is not the same as “complete and accurate engineering truth.” Here’s why even that article’s statement doesn’t undo the need for quarantine: ### 1. The article is describing the most likely scenario, not the only one When you’re on a tech support site, you’re usually reading about a computer that is already exhibiting symptoms—pop-ups, slowdowns, redirects. In that context, a flagged file is almost certainly malicious, and the article is trying to reassure you to take action. But in the broader, invisible background of millions of daily scans, the antivirus is making constant judgment calls on files that are *not* currently causing symptoms, on machines that are running perfectly. That’s where false positives lurk. ### 2. Even major antivirus companies openly warn about false positives If flagged files were malware “plain and simple,” then every AV vendor’s own documentation wouldn’t have entire sections on how false positives happen and what to do about them. They all have processes for users to submit files they believe were wrongly flagged. They wouldn’t build those systems if the problem didn’t exist. A simple tech support article might skip that nuance, but the actual engineers who build the software know it’s a reality. ### 3. There are famous, well-documented cases of massive false positives Just to prove it’s not th